# API entrypoints

Odroe is one Dart package with product-first public libraries. Import the
smallest entrypoint that owns the behavior; platform entrypoints re-export
their neutral contract where useful.

## Application and Flutter

| Entrypoint | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `odroe.dart` | `AppContext`, `Module`, typed bindings, registry lifecycle |
| `odroe_flutter.dart` | Flutter `App`, bindings, and the common `QueryModule`, `RpcModule`, `DocumentModule`, and `RouterModule` composition surface |
| `router.dart` | Typed routes, codecs, matching, loads, URL generation |
| `router_flutter.dart` | Flutter Router module, pages, shells, navigation |

Application `main.dart` can import only `odroe_flutter.dart` for the common
composition modules. Route pages, custom Query clients, RPC transports, server
hosts, and database implementations should still import the narrow entrypoint
that owns their behavior.

## Query and RPC

| Entrypoint | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `query.dart` | Cache, keys, query/mutation/infinite lifecycles, hydration, persistence contracts |
| `query_flutter.dart` | `QueryBuilder`, infinite/mutation builders, Flutter module/provider |
| `rpc.dart` | Generated refs, input encoders, output decoders, client, transport, serializer, cancellation, `RpcModule` |

`rpc.dart` exports the `http` package's `Client` interface needed by
`HttpTransport`; implement `BaseClient` in application tests or adapters when a
custom client is required.

`ValueEncoder<T>` and `ValueDecoder<T>` are the small runtime hooks used by
generated record codecs. Application code normally consumes the typed
`ServerFunctionRef` and does not construct these callbacks itself. A bad typed
output frame is reported as `RpcProtocolException`.

`QueryKey<T>` binds one canonical query-cache identity to one exact data type.
`T` does not affect equality, JSON, or prefix matching, but a same-canonical
type conflict throws `StateError` without replacing the active query entry.
When no entry exists, hydration restores an untyped placeholder. Completed data
is checked before typed adoption; pending options may adopt immediately and the
transferred Future is checked when it completes. A mismatch then leaves that
typed query in error. Mutation keys are operation identities and do not bind a
mutation result type. Standalone query keys should declare `T`; standalone
prefixes should use `QueryKey<Object?>`.

## Server hosts

| Entrypoint | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `server.dart` | Platform-neutral application core, `Server`, routes, functions, middleware, requests/responses, controlled errors |
| `server_io.dart` | `dart:io` HTTP adapter and prerendering |
| `server_fetch.dart` | Fetch adapter, invocation bindings, export helper |

Server route files normally import only `server.dart`; it also owns the
`Module`, `AppContext`, and binding types used by server composition. Client code
imports `rpc.dart`; Fetch and IO imports stay at their host entrypoints.

## Data

| Entrypoint | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `database.dart` | Typed SQL contracts, codecs, rows, statements, modules, relational queries |
| `database_sqlite.dart` | Native `SqliteDatabase` |
| `database_postgres.dart` | `PostgresDatabase` plus public connection/pool constructor types |
| `database_mysql.dart` | Native `MysqlDatabase` for MySQL/MariaDB |
| `database_d1.dart` | Cloudflare `D1SqlDatabase` |

Do not import native drivers into shared route or Worker code. Put the driver in
the runtime-specific server entry and expose only `DatabaseModule` to handlers.

### Typed SQL terminals

| Terminal | Result contract |
| --- | --- |
| `SqlRead.all(executor)` | Decodes every returned row into a list |
| `SqlRead.one(executor)` | Returns exactly one row; rejects zero or multiple rows |
| `SqlRead.oneOrNull(executor)` | Returns one row or `null`; rejects multiple rows |

For typed selects, `one` and `oneOrNull` execute a query capped at two rows so
they can reject duplicates without transferring an unbounded result. Limits of
zero, one, or two and every valid offset are preserved; a larger or omitted
limit becomes two for the cardinality probe. A mutation with
`RETURNING` cannot be capped portably, so the database applies the mutation
before Odroe checks all returned rows or decodes the projection. Use an
interactive transaction where the driver supports one when either failure must
roll back the write. D1 has no interactive transaction callback, so its SQL
predicate or schema constraint must guarantee the expected row count instead.

When the projection type itself is nullable, `oneOrNull` cannot distinguish no
row from one row that decodes to `null`. Use `one` or `all` when presence must
remain observable.

### Typed SQL writes

| Builder | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| `SqlQueries.insert(table, values)` | Inserts one row; empty values use the dialect's default-row syntax |
| `SqlQueries.insertMany(table, rows)` | Inserts non-empty, identically shaped rows in one statement |
| `SqlQueries.insertOnConflictDoNothing(table, values, target: columns)` | Inserts one row or ignores the named unique/primary-key conflict |
| `SqlQueries.updateWhere` / `deleteWhere` | Requires an explicit predicate |
| `SqlQueries.updateAll` / `deleteAll` | Requires the visible `allRows` confirmation token |

Conflict-target inserts require non-empty values and target columns owned by
the same table. The database, not `SqlTable`, remains the authority for whether
that column list is an accepted conflict arbiter; use `BoundSql` for other index
shapes. SQLite, D1, and PostgreSQL support the operation. `SqlDialect.mysql`
returns `SqlErrorCode.unsupported` before reading the input iterables because
MySQL has no equivalent target-specific contract.
`SqlWrite.returning(projection)` remains available where the dialect supports
`RETURNING`; an ignored conflict returns no row.

`insertMany` requires at least one row and at least one assignment per row, then
validates the complete input before SQL construction. Rows must use the same
table, contain no duplicate columns, and keep the first row's exact column
objects and order. It does not auto-chunk; callers own provider parameter
limits. SQLite, D1, and PostgreSQL support `RETURNING`, while MySQL/MariaDB does
not. Returned rows have no portable input-order guarantee, so associate them by
a stable key rather than list index.

## Documents and content

| Entrypoint | Owns |
| --- | --- |
| `document.dart` | Semantic HTML nodes, route documents, renderer, server integration |
| `document_flutter.dart` | Flutter handoff and Document module |
| `mdc.dart` | MDC AST, parser, components, semantic HTML, outline |
| `mdc_flutter.dart` | Flutter MDC renderer, controller, styles, view |
| `press.dart` | Immutable typed content pages and collections |
| `press_io.dart` | File-system content discovery for server/build tools |

`press_io.dart`, `server_io.dart`, SQLite, PostgreSQL, and MySQL are not Web
libraries. Conditional exports should make the platform boundary explicit.

This page documents the current public entrypoints, not every exported member. Use
the API docs generated from the package source for signatures, then return to
the concept and guide pages for ownership rules.
